WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.130 --> 00:00:04.460
Speaker 1: It's great to have back on the
program today Dean Baker, who's a senior economist

00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:09.260
at the Center for Economic and Policy Research
and also a visiting professor at the University

00:00:09.260 --> 00:00:10.410
of Utah.

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:18.070
Dean, we spoke about a year ago, most recently,
and we now have a very different situation

00:00:18.070 --> 00:00:22.010
from the standpoint of the pandemic economically.

00:00:22.010 --> 00:00:25.949
What are you most paying attention to and
what is of concern at this point?

00:00:25.949 --> 00:00:31.349
Speaker 5: Well, the economy is recovering
rapidly and that that's a very good story.

00:00:31.349 --> 00:00:33.050
I mean, we still have a lot of people out
of work.

00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:37.980
We're still not back to what we might consider
normal, certainly far from full employment.

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:41.860
But to my view, the question is the rate at
which we're going and the extent to which

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:42.860
we see obstacles.

00:00:42.860 --> 00:00:46.830
One of the big things or the big thing, of
course, is the pandemic itself.

00:00:46.830 --> 00:00:52.860
I mean, we have the numbers way, way down
because primarily because of the vaccines,

00:00:52.860 --> 00:00:54.170
which have been great.

00:00:54.170 --> 00:00:57.500
But we know there's a lot of people who don't
want to take them.

00:00:57.500 --> 00:00:59.980
And as a result of that, they're getting sick
there.

00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:01.800
So that's a bad story.

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:05.600
Now, as it stands, I don't think there's a
big economic problem with that.

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:08.079
I mean, I don't want to trivialize people
getting sick and dying.

00:01:08.079 --> 00:01:09.079
That's a big deal.

00:01:09.079 --> 00:01:12.340
But I mean, if we're just talking about the
economic impact, I think that's relatively

00:01:12.340 --> 00:01:13.340
limited.

00:01:13.340 --> 00:01:17.299
The big concern and this should be a huge
warning to us.

00:01:17.299 --> 00:01:21.130
We, of course, are talking about the Delta
variant and the vaccines are still affected

00:01:21.130 --> 00:01:22.130
from that.

00:01:22.130 --> 00:01:25.560
But as long as the pandemic is spreading out
of control, not here.

00:01:25.560 --> 00:01:30.340
I mean, obviously, we care about here, too,
but around the world, we're not going to be

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:31.340
safe.

00:01:31.340 --> 00:01:32.970
And that's a really, really big deal.

00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:38.020
So I wished we had done I wish we still now
do more to get the world vaccinated.

00:01:38.020 --> 00:01:40.369
So that's a really, really big story.

00:01:40.369 --> 00:01:43.299
But the other part, just how do things look
in the economy right now?

00:01:43.299 --> 00:01:48.470
Well, we've had fantastic job growth the last
four month, over six hundred thousand a month.

00:01:48.470 --> 00:01:50.920
We're going to we'll get GDP report next week.

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:55.179
And the second quarter's almost certainly
going to put us above where we were pre pandemic.

00:01:55.179 --> 00:02:00.350
I mean, we have a ways to go yet to be back
on trend, but most things are looking pretty

00:02:00.350 --> 00:02:01.529
good.

00:02:01.529 --> 00:02:05.740
And so, you know, as the economy goes, I'm
feeling pretty good about it.

00:02:05.740 --> 00:02:10.210
You know, we just have to make sure we could
slow the growth of the pandemic, partly here,

00:02:10.210 --> 00:02:12.460
but probably more importantly, right now around
the world,

00:02:12.460 --> 00:02:16.400
Speaker 1: there's been this refrain or talking
point.

00:02:16.400 --> 00:02:22.480
Call it what you will in the context of this
enhanced unemployment benefit that the federal

00:02:22.480 --> 00:02:25.020
government established during the pandemic.

00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:27.300
That now is things are opening.

00:02:27.300 --> 00:02:29.070
There's a worker shortage.

00:02:29.070 --> 00:02:34.000
Businesses are having trouble finding people
to work because people, quote, would rather

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:36.540
sit at home and collect their enhanced unemployment.

00:02:36.540 --> 00:02:42.510
Now, we have some early data from some states
that decided to get ahead of Joe Biden September

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:45.960
date and just cut the enhanced unemployment
early.

00:02:45.960 --> 00:02:52.970
It's not totally clear that it's really having
a definitive impact, actually, on the rate

00:02:52.970 --> 00:02:56.420
at which people are starting to look for jobs
or being hired.

00:02:56.420 --> 00:03:02.730
Generally speaking, my view is most people
want to work and most people find, for better

00:03:02.730 --> 00:03:05.901
or worse, they find meaning and identity through
work.

00:03:05.901 --> 00:03:07.500
And that could be a bad thing or a good thing.

00:03:07.500 --> 00:03:11.660
We could talk about that, but that the kind
of free rider, the people who just would prefer

00:03:11.660 --> 00:03:16.650
to sit at home and not work are so, so small
in number that they're not significant.

00:03:16.650 --> 00:03:18.280
What's your sense of that talking point?

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:22.230
Speaker 5: Yeah, well, you know, everyone's
got their lazy cousin who's sitting at home

00:03:22.230 --> 00:03:26.110
collecting benefits and they extrapolate to
the whole world and that's how many lives

00:03:26.110 --> 00:03:27.110
out there actually exist.

00:03:27.110 --> 00:03:31.120
But in any case, you know, everyone could
say they know someone and some people surely

00:03:31.120 --> 00:03:32.120
are.

00:03:32.120 --> 00:03:33.950
But there's been a lot of research on this.

00:03:33.950 --> 00:03:38.070
And I remember we had six hundred dollars
a week suppliments back in the original curve's

00:03:38.070 --> 00:03:39.070
act.

00:03:39.070 --> 00:03:40.070
Right.

00:03:40.070 --> 00:03:41.440
And the number of efforts to study the impact
of that.

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:43.460
And they found it was very little, wasn't
zero.

00:03:43.460 --> 00:03:48.160
I mean, obviously there's some people that
will make a difference for but there was relatively

00:03:48.160 --> 00:03:49.160
little impact.

00:03:49.160 --> 00:03:53.590
And you mentioned that now you have several
states that have jumped the gun or whatever

00:03:53.590 --> 00:03:54.590
term you want to use.

00:03:54.590 --> 00:03:57.910
They've suspended ended the benefits early.

00:03:57.910 --> 00:04:02.560
And my friend Aaron Bass at the University
of Massachusetts analyzed what the performance

00:04:02.560 --> 00:04:07.000
has been in the states that have been to benefits
early compared to other states.

00:04:07.000 --> 00:04:08.060
And there's no difference.

00:04:08.060 --> 00:04:09.890
In fact, I think there's a very small uptick.

00:04:09.890 --> 00:04:11.450
I mean, I'm not going to blame it on that.

00:04:11.450 --> 00:04:15.970
But I think they actually had somewhat higher
unemployment and a rise in unemployment relative

00:04:15.970 --> 00:04:17.260
to other states.

00:04:17.260 --> 00:04:21.920
So the idea that that's a major factor in
keeping people from looking for work, I just

00:04:21.920 --> 00:04:23.250
don't buy it.

00:04:23.250 --> 00:04:27.430
What I do think is going on is you have a
lot of businesses really opening at the same

00:04:27.430 --> 00:04:28.430
time.

00:04:28.430 --> 00:04:29.720
And the biggest story, of course, is restaurants.

00:04:29.720 --> 00:04:31.920
That's the one that was hardest hit.

00:04:31.920 --> 00:04:33.470
And now they're reopening.

00:04:33.470 --> 00:04:36.950
So they're looking all hire a lot of workers
at the same time.

00:04:36.950 --> 00:04:41.100
And it shouldn't be surprising that that's
it's not that easy that that, you know, they're

00:04:41.100 --> 00:04:42.100
in a situation.

00:04:42.100 --> 00:04:43.220
They can't get the workers they need.

00:04:43.220 --> 00:04:44.220
They're bidding up wages.

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:45.750
I mean, that's a good thing in my view.

00:04:45.750 --> 00:04:50.660
But in any case, that's really not a terribly
surprising story, nor necessarily a bad story.

00:04:50.660 --> 00:04:55.260
And in any case, it doesn't seem as though
the unemployment supplements are a big part

00:04:55.260 --> 00:04:56.260
of the picture.

00:04:56.260 --> 00:04:59.430
Speaker 1: Is it as simple as some others
are saying, which is.

00:04:59.430 --> 00:05:00.430
Well.

00:05:00.430 --> 00:05:07.130
It's just now the businesses that the employers
need to compete for, the apparently temporarily

00:05:07.130 --> 00:05:11.930
limited pool of potential workers, and this
should just mean that they pay more and then

00:05:11.930 --> 00:05:13.230
it'll solve everybody's problem.

00:05:13.230 --> 00:05:14.340
Is it that simple?

00:05:14.340 --> 00:05:15.800
Speaker 5: It is.

00:05:15.800 --> 00:05:20.310
I think that's basically a story in some business
to say that, well, I can't afford to pay higher

00:05:20.310 --> 00:05:21.310
wages.

00:05:21.310 --> 00:05:22.520
And I'm sure in some cases that's true.

00:05:22.520 --> 00:05:26.110
And, you know, look, this is a capitalist
economy.

00:05:26.110 --> 00:05:29.790
And, you know, some businesses, businesses
go out of business all the time.

00:05:29.790 --> 00:05:34.090
I mean, it sure was that I mean, I know people
put their pulao work and many cases their

00:05:34.090 --> 00:05:38.770
life savings, you know, so so I don't mean
to trivialize that, but that is the reality.

00:05:38.770 --> 00:05:44.200
So in some cases, we'll have situations where
businesses just aren't going to be profitable.

00:05:44.200 --> 00:05:49.210
And I understand they don't want to say, well,
if I get away with paying workers 10 an hour,

00:05:49.210 --> 00:05:53.280
15 things, but sorry, you know, that's that's
going to be the world.

00:05:53.280 --> 00:05:58.590
And so so I just don't see this as the big
crisis that's being portrayed as

00:05:58.590 --> 00:06:04.600
Speaker 1: on the other side of this is pricing
and inflation.

00:06:04.600 --> 00:06:10.370
During the pandemic, we saw really significant
increases in some products.

00:06:10.370 --> 00:06:15.730
Lumber was a big one that had some sort of
follow on effects in terms of building of

00:06:15.730 --> 00:06:19.410
new construction, homes, renovations, et cetera.

00:06:19.410 --> 00:06:23.660
The price of lumber has now actually dropped
precipitously, although that drop does not

00:06:23.660 --> 00:06:28.140
yet seem to have filtered through as much
as it maybe will to the end.

00:06:28.140 --> 00:06:33.780
Products that the lumber is is used for some
of the inflation data that we see compared,

00:06:33.780 --> 00:06:37.130
for example, May twenty one to May 20.

00:06:37.130 --> 00:06:41.390
So you're sort of comparing to when there
was a significant demand collapse and maybe

00:06:41.390 --> 00:06:44.020
that's not the right way to look at it.

00:06:44.020 --> 00:06:46.430
What's your perspective on this issue of inflation?

00:06:46.430 --> 00:06:50.630
Speaker 5: Well, I think for the most part,
it's reasonably well under control, so again,

00:06:50.630 --> 00:06:53.540
the way to make comparison are now our June
to June.

00:06:53.540 --> 00:06:57.139
But I mean, that's you know, it's kind of
silly because it's you know, these are months

00:06:57.139 --> 00:06:58.610
where prices were badly depressed.

00:06:58.610 --> 00:07:04.730
So if you look at what inflation's done since
February, the last prepend month, it's up.

00:07:04.730 --> 00:07:06.010
So there's no doubt about it.

00:07:06.010 --> 00:07:10.479
But an overwhelmingly large share of that
is simply cars, both new and used cars.

00:07:10.479 --> 00:07:12.610
And we know what the story is there.

00:07:12.610 --> 00:07:18.199
There was a fire in a semiconductor factory
in Japan, and that's led to a worldwide shortage

00:07:18.199 --> 00:07:23.199
of semiconductors, which has limited the production
of new cars.

00:07:23.199 --> 00:07:28.740
And what that's mean is people have money
now from the various pandemic programs.

00:07:28.740 --> 00:07:32.229
They want to buy a car, new cars that they
can't get a new car.

00:07:32.229 --> 00:07:35.820
So they buy a used car and use car prices
have just gone through the roof.

00:07:35.820 --> 00:07:38.760
They've actually begun to fall by some measures
as of June.

00:07:38.760 --> 00:07:41.290
So we'll see how that pans out over the rest
of the year.

00:07:41.290 --> 00:07:44.190
But that's the bulk of the uptick in inflation.

00:07:44.190 --> 00:07:47.290
So it's hard to get too concerned about that.

00:07:47.290 --> 00:07:51.490
My expectation is that we're going to see
those prices falling through the rest of the

00:07:51.490 --> 00:07:52.490
year.

00:07:52.490 --> 00:07:54.150
I mean, they'll still be higher than a two
20 level, but they're going to be falling

00:07:54.150 --> 00:07:56.500
from where they were in June.

00:07:56.500 --> 00:07:58.370
So I'm not that concerned about that.

00:07:58.370 --> 00:08:03.590
Other areas of The New York Times that a big
piece on housing house prices, sale prices

00:08:03.590 --> 00:08:04.780
have risen a lot.

00:08:04.780 --> 00:08:09.350
And that's because a lot of people are moving
that we have very low mortgage interest rates

00:08:09.350 --> 00:08:13.509
and you have a lot of people that are moving
from expensive places like New York, Washington,

00:08:13.509 --> 00:08:17.880
where I came from, and they're moving to cheaper
places around the country and pushing putting

00:08:17.880 --> 00:08:20.710
a big upward pressure on prices there.

00:08:20.710 --> 00:08:22.920
New York and some of the more expensive ones.

00:08:22.920 --> 00:08:24.280
It's price growth is slowing.

00:08:24.280 --> 00:08:25.820
In some cases they're actually going negative.

00:08:25.820 --> 00:08:28.610
But, you know, but that's that's a big story.

00:08:28.610 --> 00:08:33.460
Now, whether that leads to sustained increase
in rents, I think it's a little too early

00:08:33.460 --> 00:08:34.460
to tell.

00:08:34.460 --> 00:08:37.120
I was struck and I know this isn't necessarily
a good story, but if we're trying to sell

00:08:37.120 --> 00:08:42.390
the picture of the housing market, we have
to consider we're ending the the eviction

00:08:42.390 --> 00:08:45.790
moratorium ends at the end of this month.

00:08:45.790 --> 00:08:49.600
And there are reports, I think these are exaggerated,
that somewhere around seven or eight million

00:08:49.600 --> 00:08:50.670
people face eviction.

00:08:50.670 --> 00:08:54.640
I don't think anything like that will actually
be evicted, but a lot of people will be.

00:08:54.640 --> 00:08:57.000
You know, again, I don't I'm not saying it's
a good thing.

00:08:57.000 --> 00:09:01.190
I'm just saying if we're looking at the housing
market and let's say we have 800000 people

00:09:01.190 --> 00:09:03.140
evict, I'm pulling that someone out better.

00:09:03.140 --> 00:09:07.010
But it will be a lot that's a lot of vacant
units.

00:09:07.010 --> 00:09:10.680
So, you know, it's again, I don't mean to
say any of this is good.

00:09:10.680 --> 00:09:12.010
That isn't.

00:09:12.010 --> 00:09:16.779
But we have a lot of turmoil in the economy
right now, which has led to inflation in some

00:09:16.779 --> 00:09:20.040
areas, you know, as mentioned in cars that
could identify some others.

00:09:20.040 --> 00:09:26.190
But it's not an underlying it's not the sort
of inflation we saw in the 70s where you had

00:09:26.190 --> 00:09:30.010
higher wages, driving higher prices, leading
workers to push for higher wages.

00:09:30.010 --> 00:09:31.480
And then we got the spiraling upward.

00:09:31.480 --> 00:09:33.970
We're very, very far from that story.

00:09:33.970 --> 00:09:40.880
Speaker 1: You mentioned in the car discussion
that now part of this is people have money

00:09:40.880 --> 00:09:44.080
to spend and they want cars as they get back
to things.

00:09:44.080 --> 00:09:49.350
One of the interesting things that we saw,
so to kind of set this up pre pandemic, frequently,

00:09:49.350 --> 00:09:54.600
we would see articles with headlines like
40 percent of Americans would need to borrow

00:09:54.600 --> 00:09:57.930
to meet an unexpected 400 dollar expense.

00:09:57.930 --> 00:10:03.750
And about the the point of those stories was
very low savings rate in the United States

00:10:03.750 --> 00:10:04.820
during the pandemic.

00:10:04.820 --> 00:10:11.430
We saw headlines about dramatically higher
savings rate in the United States at a macroeconomic

00:10:11.430 --> 00:10:12.430
level.

00:10:12.430 --> 00:10:13.690
How should we think about that?

00:10:13.690 --> 00:10:17.860
Because there's a school of thought that keeping
money in bank accounts instead of spending

00:10:17.860 --> 00:10:22.270
it has a very low economic multiplier and
is not so good for the economy.

00:10:22.270 --> 00:10:25.529
Although at the individual level, certainly
we can understand how having an emergency

00:10:25.529 --> 00:10:28.430
fund is a very good thing for a household
budget.

00:10:28.430 --> 00:10:30.290
So how should we be thinking about that?

00:10:30.290 --> 00:10:32.380
Speaker 5: Well, there's two issues here.

00:10:32.380 --> 00:10:36.700
One is if we're thinking of just immediate
demand in the economy, that does restrain

00:10:36.700 --> 00:10:37.700
demand.

00:10:37.700 --> 00:10:43.060
So if I have got my twelve hundred or fourteen
hundred dollar check from President Biden

00:10:43.060 --> 00:10:46.709
and go, OK, well, I'm put putting the banks,
I need to spend it right now.

00:10:46.709 --> 00:10:50.870
Well, if we're worried about inflation, that's
actually a good thing.

00:10:50.870 --> 00:10:54.140
So so if we're worried, there's going to be
too much demand in the economy, that's a good

00:10:54.140 --> 00:10:55.140
thing.

00:10:55.140 --> 00:10:57.520
So I think he has that has worked out.

00:10:57.520 --> 00:11:02.480
It's basically been a positive for the economy
in the sense that is we're having this reopening

00:11:02.480 --> 00:11:05.240
and there's a lot of stress in different areas.

00:11:05.240 --> 00:11:09.830
It probably is a good thing that everyone
does and rush out and spending it now, say,

00:11:09.830 --> 00:11:11.240
six months a year down the road.

00:11:11.240 --> 00:11:15.380
As you say, the flip side, the micro side
is, OK, I've got money in the bank.

00:11:15.380 --> 00:11:16.380
I feel better.

00:11:16.380 --> 00:11:17.900
And we know I mean, there's a lot of data.

00:11:17.900 --> 00:11:20.370
People's balance sheets are better than they've
been in decades.

00:11:20.370 --> 00:11:21.370
Right.

00:11:21.370 --> 00:11:22.960
So things really look much better.

00:11:22.960 --> 00:11:27.920
So that's great both in their security, but
also maybe they want to do a big ticket purchase.

00:11:27.920 --> 00:11:32.300
Maybe they're going to use that money as a
down payment on a house or a car or whatever

00:11:32.300 --> 00:11:33.540
it might be.

00:11:33.540 --> 00:11:38.620
So I can say they're overwhelmingly positive
that, again, if we were worried, oh, we need

00:11:38.620 --> 00:11:44.110
more demand in the economy right now, then
people saving is definitely a bad thing.

00:11:44.110 --> 00:11:45.902
But we're not in that situation, or at least
I don't.

00:11:45.902 --> 00:11:50.340
I believe we're in that situation, so I'm
very happy to see that a lot of people are

00:11:50.340 --> 00:11:54.250
in a situation where they could save some
of their money and again, they could always

00:11:54.250 --> 00:11:57.830
keep it there as a reserve or they may decide
six months, a year down the road to have a

00:11:57.830 --> 00:11:59.860
big ticket purchase to other.

00:11:59.860 --> 00:12:04.440
Speaker 1: I don't know if we would call them
trends or things that have kind of been topics

00:12:04.440 --> 00:12:06.750
of conversation as a result of the pandemic.

00:12:06.750 --> 00:12:13.420
One is work from home and the different decisions
that are being taken by decisions being made

00:12:13.420 --> 00:12:18.890
by corporations about we will do permanent,
possible work from home or we will do a split

00:12:18.890 --> 00:12:20.850
of work from home and be back in the office,
etc..

00:12:20.850 --> 00:12:23.080
That's one trend.

00:12:23.080 --> 00:12:27.190
The other one is we're also getting more and
more information from these four day a week

00:12:27.190 --> 00:12:33.800
work trials, which sometimes are a full 40
hours, but over four days, and sometimes they're

00:12:33.800 --> 00:12:37.990
actually fewer hours, like a 36 or thirty
seven hour week over four days.

00:12:37.990 --> 00:12:42.560
And looking at what employee productivity
is over that period of time rather than forty

00:12:42.560 --> 00:12:44.220
or forty five hours.

00:12:44.220 --> 00:12:50.279
What's your thought on these things at sort
of this point in time, but also bigger picture.

00:12:50.279 --> 00:12:54.890
Speaker 5: I think they're both very encouraging
trends, taking one at a time.

00:12:54.890 --> 00:12:59.950
Yeah, the work home, clearly we're going to
see way, way more work from home than than

00:12:59.950 --> 00:13:01.400
we did pre pandemic.

00:13:01.400 --> 00:13:07.589
I mean, the point is, you know, business inertia
is an incredibly powerful force in the economy.

00:13:07.589 --> 00:13:09.530
People have to appreciate that for a long
time.

00:13:09.530 --> 00:13:13.060
As an economist ago, maybe it should be like
this rather than that and go, well, maybe

00:13:13.060 --> 00:13:14.709
it should be, but it's not going to be.

00:13:14.709 --> 00:13:15.709
Well, right.

00:13:15.709 --> 00:13:20.630
Once employers got used to having people working
from home and they kept their business operating,

00:13:20.630 --> 00:13:21.900
then they can't say it doesn't work.

00:13:21.900 --> 00:13:23.090
They know it works.

00:13:23.090 --> 00:13:27.279
So we are going to see many more situations
where either people are totally working from

00:13:27.279 --> 00:13:31.850
home or cases where maybe they come in once
or twice a week from the office.

00:13:31.850 --> 00:13:34.890
And I think that's a great thing from a number
of different perspectives.

00:13:34.890 --> 00:13:41.640
Obviously, it saves enormous amount of resources,
commuting people's time.

00:13:41.640 --> 00:13:43.750
I mean, obviously, people value their time.

00:13:43.750 --> 00:13:49.029
And if you're commuting two hours a day, one
hour each way that you're wasting your time

00:13:49.029 --> 00:13:50.029
on anyone.

00:13:50.029 --> 00:13:51.029
I mean, there's a lot of things we could argue.

00:13:51.029 --> 00:13:52.680
How much do you like this or that?

00:13:52.680 --> 00:13:55.779
And when you watch their commute and, you
know, I'm sure someone somewhere does.

00:13:55.779 --> 00:13:59.529
Speaker 1: But that's I think I think just
to interject one idea there.

00:13:59.529 --> 00:14:05.800
There are some studies about if you have a
15 minute commute that it seems sort of for

00:14:05.800 --> 00:14:10.490
a lot of people to be a positive kind of change
of phase from home to work.

00:14:10.490 --> 00:14:14.560
And that that, you know, once you get longer
than that, the duration of it seems to start

00:14:14.560 --> 00:14:16.500
having like a more of a net negative effect.

00:14:16.500 --> 00:14:20.970
Speaker 5: Yeah, I believe that I mean, a
short enough commute I can grant isn't a big

00:14:20.970 --> 00:14:22.660
deal, you know, just to walk to work.

00:14:22.660 --> 00:14:26.250
I was lucky enough for for a period of time
to be able to walk to work.

00:14:26.250 --> 00:14:27.990
It was maybe half mile, so.

00:14:27.990 --> 00:14:28.990
Yeah.

00:14:28.990 --> 00:14:29.990
And, you know, ten minutes or so.

00:14:29.990 --> 00:14:31.220
So, yeah, that was fine.

00:14:31.220 --> 00:14:32.430
That that didn't bother me at first.

00:14:32.430 --> 00:14:35.380
I think the vast majority of commuters are
considerably more time, so.

00:14:35.380 --> 00:14:36.380
Yeah.

00:14:36.380 --> 00:14:39.720
So I think that's a great thing and I think
it's it's good, of course, from environmental

00:14:39.720 --> 00:14:41.940
perspective, you know, where people don't.

00:14:41.940 --> 00:14:45.180
Obviously a lot of resources are wasted in
commuting.

00:14:45.180 --> 00:14:47.779
So so that's a great story.

00:14:47.779 --> 00:14:49.370
Four day work week, I think.

00:14:49.370 --> 00:14:50.370
Similar story.

00:14:50.370 --> 00:14:55.459
Again, if you compare the US to Europe, there
have been sharp reductions in work hours over

00:14:55.459 --> 00:14:56.459
the last four decades.

00:14:56.459 --> 00:15:02.470
And I was going to say most all European countries
and we now are average work year in the United

00:15:02.470 --> 00:15:07.240
States, twenty, twenty five percent longer
than it is in Germany and Netherlands, Denmark,

00:15:07.240 --> 00:15:08.710
other wealthy countries.

00:15:08.710 --> 00:15:11.630
And I think most people do value their time.

00:15:11.630 --> 00:15:15.620
And if we could have a four day workweek rather
than a five, that's great.

00:15:15.620 --> 00:15:19.710
And yeah, I'd love to see the hours cut because
I think a 10 hour day, certainly in a lot

00:15:19.710 --> 00:15:22.260
of jobs, people would find exhausting.

00:15:22.260 --> 00:15:26.380
So if we can make it a nine hour day, maybe
somewhere down the road, maybe an eight hour

00:15:26.380 --> 00:15:29.620
day, but whatever, nine hours isn't that much
worse than eight.

00:15:29.620 --> 00:15:32.639
So, you know, I think most people would consider
that a pretty good deal.

00:15:32.639 --> 00:15:36.410
And again, getting back to the commuting,
if you have for commute rather than five,

00:15:36.410 --> 00:15:38.200
that's that's a plus.

00:15:38.200 --> 00:15:42.270
So I'd love to see us get to to shorter work
years.

00:15:42.270 --> 00:15:43.710
And a four day workweek is really good.

00:15:43.710 --> 00:15:46.279
Way to go,
Speaker 1: without a doubt.

00:15:46.279 --> 00:15:47.660
I'm with you there, Dean.

00:15:47.660 --> 00:15:52.160
We've been speaking with Dean Baker, who's
a senior economist at the Center for Economic

00:15:52.160 --> 00:15:56.490
and Policy Research, also a visiting professor
at the University of Utah.

00:15:56.490 --> 00:15:57.490
Always great having you on.

00:15:57.490 --> 00:15:59.889
Speaker 5: Well, thanks for having me on.

00:15:59.889 --> 00:16:00.510
Good questions.

